{"id": "x0422", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 7, "onset_chunk": 6, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so if Ethel has eight dollars, then twice that amount would be 16 dollars. And Jimmy has two dollars more than that, which means he has 18 dollars. So Jimmy is having 18 dollars.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 13, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0430", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 28, "onset_chunk": 26, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, let's figure out how many red rose s Fred needs to deliver. Sand ra ordered four times as many red roses as white carn ations, and she also got 2 00 pink calla li lies, which are five times the number of white carnations. So first, let' s find out how many white carnations she order ed. If 20 0 pink calla lilies are five times the white carnations, then the white carnations must be 200 divided by 5, which is 40. Now that we know there were 4 0 white carnations, the red roses are four times that amount. So 4 times 40 equal s 16 0. That means Fred has to deliver 1 60 red roses by 5 p.m. to keep Sandra happy.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 39, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0438", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 12, "onset_chunk": 11, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Let's call the amount the first person took x. The second person took 80 dollars more than the first, so that's x plus 8 0. The third person took twice what the second took, which is 2 times x plus 80. Now, we know all three amounts add up to 19 20 dollars. So let' s write that equation: x plus x plus 8 0 plus 2 times x plus 80 equal s 1 9 2 0. Let' s simplify it step by step. First, combine like terms in the parentheses: 2 times x plus 80 is 2x plus 1 60. Now the equation is x plus x plus 80 plus 2 x plus 1 60 equals 1 92 0. Comb ine the x terms: x plus x plus 2 x is 4 x. Com bine the constant term s: 80 plus 16 0 is 2 40. So now we have 4x plus 2 40 equal s 1 9 20. Sub tract 2", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0446", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 13, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Eva has saved 2 0 dollars. An tony has saved ten dollars more than Eva, so that's twenty plus ten, which is thirty dollars. Now, Roy has saved forty percent more than Anthony. Four ty percent of thirty is twelve, because thirty times zero point four is twelve. So, adding that to Anthony' s thirty dollars, Roy has saved forty two dollars in total.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 20, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0454", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 13, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So she needs to drink 60 milliliters per kilometer. Each lap is 0. 25 kilometers, so that means each lap she needs to drink 15 mill iliters of water because 6 0 times 0.2 5 equals 15. She' s running eight laps, so she needs to drink a total of 1 20 mill iters of water because 1 5 times 8 equal s 120.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 18, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0462", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 18, "onset_chunk": 17, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So there are 6 0 questions total. 4 0% of them are easy, so that's 2 4 questions. The rest are equally divided between average and difficult, so each category has 18 questions. A ries gets 7 5% of the easy ones right, which is 18 out of 24. For the average and difficult questions, she gets half correct, so that's 9 out of 18 for each group. Add ing it up: 18 plus 9 plus 9 gives her a total of 3 6 points.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 32, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0470", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 23, "onset_chunk": 22, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Let's break this down step by step. We know the month has 30 days total. Over those 30 days, she had 12 good days, 8 bad days, and the rest were neutral. So first, let' s figure out how many neutral days there are. That would be 3 0 minus 12 good minus 8 bad, which is 10 neutral days.Now, looking at her pattern: the first eight days were all good. The second eight days were all bad. And the third eight days were all neutral. But wait a minute—eight plus eight plus eight is already 24 days, and we only have 30 days in the month. So actually, after the first twenty- four days, there are six more days left. The problem says that the next three days are good, neutral, and good. So let's assume these happen right after the first twenty-four days. That means on day 25, it' s good; day 26 is neutral; and day 2 7 is good again. Now, remember, originally she had twelve good days planned for the whole month. She used up eight of them in the first eight days. Then, when these next three days roll in, two", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0478", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 23, "onset_chunk": 23, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Mary has 4 0 window led ges and she already has two pot ted plants on each one. So that means she starts with forty times two, which is eighty potted plants in total before receiving the new ones. She then gets eighteen more, so her total becomes eighty plus eighteen, which equals ninety eight potted plants. Now, she' s giving away one plant from each of the forty ledges to friends and family. That means she gives away forty plants in all. If we take those forty away from her ninety-eight, she will be left with fifty- eight potted plants.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 32, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0486", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 14, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Sil enda bake s twice as many cookies as Greta, so she bakes 2 times 3 0 cookies, which is 60. Together, they have 30 plus 60, which is 9 0 cookies. They eat 1 0 while they cool, so 90 minus 10 is 8 0. There are 80 cookies in the box.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 16, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0494", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 34, "onset_chunk": 33, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so let' s break this down. Col by gets $1 50 a month for the movies. He already saw five Friday or Saturday movies and eight other day movies. So first, let's calculate how much he spent on those. Five Friday or Saturday movie tickets at $1 0 each is $50. Eight other day movies at $7 each is $5 6. That' s $1 06 total on tickets. Now, for snack s, he had two tubs of pop corn at $8 each, which is $1 6, and four boxes of candy at $2 each, which is $8. So snacks cost him $ 24. Adding the tickets and snack s together, that ’s $10 6 plus $2 4, which equal s $1 30. But wait, he only got $1 5 0 for the whole month, and he ’s already spent $ 1 30. That means he has no money left for tonight. However, he wants to go tonight anyway—", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0502", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 14, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Let's figure out how many colors Beatrice bought. So, first, she had 6 packet s with 8 pieces each. If we multiply 6 by 8, that gives us 48 colors from those packets. Then, for the other 4 packets, each had 16 pieces. So, 4 times 16 is 6 4 colors. Now, if we add the two amounts together, 48 plus 64 equal s 11 2. So, Bea trice bought a total of 1 12 crayon colors.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 25, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0510", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 22, "onset_chunk": 20, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so let' s figure out how much Mrs. Rylan spent on the seeds. She bought 2 0 packets of tomato seeds at $4 0 each. So for the tomatoes, that's 2 0 times 40, which equals $ 800. Then she got 8 0 packets of celery seeds at $ 30 each. That' s 80 times 3 0, which is $2, 400. Now we add the two amounts together: $ 80 0 plus $ 2,4 00 equal s $ 3,2 00. So she used $ 3,2 00 to buy all the seeds.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 33, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0518", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 10, "onset_chunk": 9, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Jenny has 12 slices to start with. She gives one third to Bill, which is 4 slice s, and one fourth to Mark, which is 3 slice s. So that' s 7 slice s given away. If she eats 2 more, that's another 9 slice s accounted for. 1 2 minus 9 leaves 3 slices left.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 16, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0526", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 9, "onset_chunk": 8, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "If George has 3 candies left after eating 5, that means he originally had 8 candies. Since Nick had twice as many as George, Nick has 16 can dies.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 11, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0534", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 18, "onset_chunk": 17, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Let's figure this out. So Mel uses a 9 00-watt air condition er for eight hours a day, which means he uses 7, 200 watt-hours per day. If he cuts back by five hours, that' s three hours of usage daily. That saves him 2,70 0 watt-hours each day. Over thirty days, that adds up to 81,0 00 watt-hours, or eighty- one kilowat t-hours saved in total.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 24, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0542", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 27, "onset_chunk": 26, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "The first dog is 10 pounds. The da xchund weighs twice as much, so that's 20 pounds. The pap illon is one-fourth of the dachshund, so that's 5 pounds. The mastiff is 44 times the papillon' s weight. So 4 4 times 5 is 2 2 0 pounds. The ma stiff weighed 2 2 0 pounds.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 22, "carrier_ended": null}
